<%# The overlay, rendered the same way on both surfaces.

    Both texts are in the DOM and one is hidden, rather than the original
    being fetched on demand: it means the original is always there to be
    found — by selection, by a screen reader, by a browser with JavaScript
    off — and there is no round trip between wanting it and seeing it.

    `label` is the noun for whoever wrote it ("what they wrote"), passed in
    rather than derived here, because the guest surface and the staff
    surface name each other differently and this partial should not need to
    know which side it is on.

    `translate` is the authorship rule, and it is the caller's to decide: a
    reader is never shown their own words translated back at them. On the
    guest surface that means anything the guest did not write; on the staff
    surface, anything staff did not write (which includes the assistant, whose
    replies go out in the guest's language). Getting this wrong is not subtle
    — it is the software telling someone what they just said.

    Locals: message, reader_locale, translate, and the two labels. %>
<% text, source = translate ? message.readable_in(reader_locale) : [ message.body, :original ] %>

<% if source == :translated %>
  <div data-controller="translation-toggle"
       data-translation-toggle-show-label-value="<%= show_label %>"
       data-translation-toggle-hide-label-value="<%= hide_label %>">
    <p data-translation-toggle-target="translated" dir="auto"><%= text %></p>
    <%# hidden, not display:none in a class — a hidden attribute is what
        assistive technology and the toggle both read, and it survives a
        stylesheet that never loaded. %>
    <p data-translation-toggle-target="original" dir="auto" hidden><%= message.body %></p>

    <button type="button" data-translation-toggle-target="button"
            data-action="click->translation-toggle#toggle"
            aria-expanded="false"
            class="mt-1 cursor-pointer text-xs font-medium underline opacity-80">
      <%= show_label %>
    </button>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <p dir="auto"><%= text %></p>

  <%# Said out loud rather than left to look like an ordinary message. A
      reader who cannot read this bubble needs to know the software tried
      and failed, not wonder why the hotel replied in a language they do not
      speak. `translating` is the in-flight case, and the watchdog
      guarantees it resolves within fifteen seconds. %>
  <% if translate && message.translation_status_failed? %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-xs opacity-70"><%= t("guest.messages.translation.untranslated_note") %></p>
  <% elsif translate && (message.translation_status_pending? || message.translation_status_translating?) %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-xs opacity-70"><%= t("guest.messages.translation.translating") %></p>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
